    <div class="distortion-block distortion-response">
      <div class="distortion-header" data-lang-en>How Islamic Scholarship Responds</div>
      <div class="distortion-header" data-lang-es style="display:none;">Cómo Responde la Erudición Islámica</div>
      <div class="distortion-body" data-lang-en>
        <p>Mainstream Islamic scholars — including authorities at Al-Azhar University (Cairo), the world's oldest and most prestigious Islamic institution — have consistently held that al-Qaeda's readings are textual distortions:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>On Quran 9:5:</strong> Classical Islamic jurisprudence reads this verse in its historical context — a specific military truce violation in 7th-century Arabia, not a standing command. The surrounding verses (9:4, 9:6) grant protection to those who honor treaties and seek asylum. Al-Azhar scholars have stated the verse cannot be read in isolation from its context.</li>
          <li><strong>On civilian immunity:</strong> Islamic law (<em>fiqh</em>) establishes explicit prohibitions against killing women, children, the elderly, merchants, and religious figures — even in declared war. The Prophet's hadith tradition (Sahih Bukhari, Sahih Muslim) records these prohibitions explicitly. The 9/11 attacks killed civilians — a category protected under traditional Islamic law regardless of their religion.</li>
          <li><strong>On suicide attacks:</strong> The Quran (4:29) prohibits self-destruction. The dominant ruling in classical Islamic scholarship is that suicide operations are forbidden (<em>haram</em>), regardless of political intent.</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="margin-top:10px;">Within days of September 11, major Islamic institutions and scholars issued condemnations:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>The Islamic Society of North America (ISNA) called the attacks "grave crimes against humanity."</li>
          <li>The Council on American-Islamic Relations (CAIR) stated: "We reiterate our unequivocal condemnation of any and all acts of terrorism and the targeting of civilians."</li>
          <li>Sheikh Yusuf Al-Qaradawi, one of the most widely followed Sunni scholars globally, issued a fatwa calling the attacks "forbidden and a major sin."</li>
          <li>King Abdullah of Jordan convened a conference of 200 Islamic scholars that produced the "Amman Message" (2004–2005), explicitly rejecting the theological basis for al-Qaeda's violence and declaring it outside the bounds of any of Islam's eight recognized legal schools.</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="margin-top:10px;font-size:12px;font-style:italic;color:var(--ink-mut);">Sources: ISNA Statement, Sept. 12, 2001; CAIR Statement, Sept. 11, 2001; Al-Qaradawi fatwa, IslamOnline, Sept. 2001; The Amman Message (ammanmessage.com), 2005; Grand Mufti of Saudi Arabia, statement Sept. 15, 2001.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="distortion-body" data-lang-es style="display:none;">
        <p>Los eruditos islámicos del mainstream — incluidas las autoridades de la Universidad Al-Azhar (El Cairo), la institución islámica más antigua y prestigiosa del mundo — han sostenido sistemáticamente que las lecturas de Al-Qaeda son distorsiones textuales de los pasajes coránicos que invocaron.</p>
        <p>Dentro de los días posteriores al 11 de septiembre, las principales instituciones y eruditos islámicos emitieron condenas. La Sociedad Islámica de América del Norte (ISNA) llamó a los ataques "crímenes graves contra la humanidad." El Consejo sobre Relaciones Americano-Islámicas (CAIR) declaró: "Reiteramos nuestra condena inequívoca de cualquier acto de terrorismo." El jeque Yusuf Al-Qaradawi emitió una fatwa llamando a los ataques "prohibidos y un pecado mayor." El Rey Abdalá de Jordania convocó una conferencia de 200 eruditos islámicos que produjo el "Mensaje de Ammán" (2004–2005), rechazando explícitamente la base teológica de la violencia de Al-Qaeda.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:10px;font-size:12px;font-style:italic;color:var(--ink-mut);">Fuentes: Declaración de ISNA, 12 sept. 2001; Declaración de CAIR, 11 sept. 2001; Fatwa de Al-Qaradawi, IslamOnline, sept. 2001; El Mensaje de Ammán (ammanmessage.com), 2005.</p>
      </div>
